<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abd al-Wahid ben Messaoud</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th–17th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Abd el-Ouahed ben Messaoud</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abd al-Wahid ben Messaoud was a Moroccan envoy of the Saadi sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mansur">Ahmad al-Mansur</a> who led an embassy to the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I of England</a> in 1600. During his six months in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> he proposed a joint Anglo-Moroccan attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, a plan that came to nothing. His portrait, painted in London in 1600, is one of the earliest known European portraits of a Muslim ambassador.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abd-al-wahid-ben-messaoud.jpg" alt="Abd al-Wahid ben Messaoud" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MoorishAmbassador_to_Elizabeth_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
